research.summary:
Both ecosystems already contain the answer inside their own SDK history, and it argues against a purely declarative hub. Extron's .pkp embeds ~2,100+ lines of live Python source per driver; Crestron's Certified Driver (.pkg, literally a renamed ZIP) embeds a JSON/.dat metadata file plus a compiled .NET DLL, and Crestron's own V1→V2 SDK evolution ("RAD Framework" → "Entity Model") was explicitly an attempt to push more of a driver into declarative data — yet V2 still requires hand-written C# method bodies behind every [EntityCommand]/[EntityProperty] attribute to actually build and send the wire command. In both vendors, the mechanically-convertible slice is real and roughly the same shape (device identity, a closed "standard command" vocabulary with string templates, transport/timing, regex-based response validation) — but in both vendors, anything outside that standard vocabulary is explicitly kicked out to imperative code ("all other standard commands must be supported in the protocol class instead," per Crestron's own docs). Recommendation: build a hub-and-spoke IR for the declarative ~60-80% (identity/metadata, standard-command/property crosswalk, transport, standard feedback grammar), but treat the imperative remainder as a first-class "escape hatch" node rather than a schema gap — and be honest that the tool's center of gravity is a source-to-source (or source-to-compiled) translator with the IR as a data sidecar, not the other way around. The sharpest asymmetry for Crestron specifically: Extron's escape hatch is interpretable Python source shipped inside the artifact (translatable, at least assistively); Crestron's is compiled IL with no source in the package (not translatable without decompilation, which is a separate legal question outside this dimension) — so an Extron→Crestron exporter for anything beyond the standard-command set isn't "fill in an IR field," it's "generate and compile new C#."

IR sketch (fields only for the part that IS mechanically convertible):
- meta: {schema_version, source{vendor, artifact_type: pkp|controlscript|ccd_v1|ccd_v2, raw_ref/hash}, certification, license_note}
- identity: {manufacturer, model, aliases[], series[], device_class (needs a cross-vendor crosswalk: Extron's asset/model taxonomy vs Crestron's closed DeviceType list vs ControlScript's free-form class name), driver_version, sdk_version, developer{company,contact,email}}
- transport: {kind: serial|tcp|udp|ssh|http|ir|cec, serial:{baud,parity,dataBits,stopBits,handshake}, ip:{port,protocol,secure,auth{type,required,defaults}}, reconnect{enabled,intervalMs}, timing:{interCommandDelayMs, responseTimeoutMs, waitForResponse}}
- capabilities[]: {id (namespaced, e.g. "power:on"), kind: command|property|event, standard_ref (maps to Extron's ExtronCommandIdEnum-style ID or Crestron's StandardCommandsEnum member when the command is in the standard set; null if custom), parameters[{name,type,range|enum,units}], custom:boolean}
- wire[]: {capability_id, direction: send|recv, template (literal/templated string, e.g. "INPUT_SELECT {input}"), delimiters, encoding}
- grammar[]: {capability_id, kind: regex|fixed-offset|delimited, pattern, group_map, priority}
- state: {properties: {id -> type/domain}, polling: [{capability_id, intervalMs, condition}]}
- escape_hatch: {kind: source|compiled-binary|none, language, entrypoint, payload_ref (inline source or opaque-blob hash), convertible: boolean}

Leak points requiring the escape hatch (not modelable as plain IR fields): (1) any command/feedback outside the vendor's own "standard" enum — explicitly the majority of real customization per Crestron's docs; (2) Crestron's runtime feature mutation (RemoveProperty/RemoveCommand + RaiseDefinitionChangedEvent) — the capability set is not fixed at load time, which breaks a static-schema IR's core assumption; (3) Crestron's DependencyGroup / cross-driver process-sharing for hot-swap — has no Extron/ControlScript analog at all; (4) Crestron's UserAttribute→SIMPL-signal metadata (Label/Description/DataType/IsPersistent/RequiredForConnection, typed as digital/analog/serial signals) — a parameter type system tied to a runtime (SIMPL) that neither Extron artifact has; (5) Extron GC's event/logic layer inside the embedded Python (per the repo's own open questions, not yet fully catalogued) is very likely to hit the same wall from the Extron side.
